[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1783","stub":{"id":"1783","section":"Hwy 70 to Hwy 27 (Whites Creek)","altname":"Tiny Piney","river":"Piney Creek","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-TNN"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1.12","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"110061","location":{"latitude":"35.8539","longitude":"-84.738"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"115319","location":{"latitude":"35.7949","longitude":"-84.7538"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"11.1","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"Kz1DOXot9azIg2lk0ecdo","title":"Half a dozen river wide strainers","post_date":"2020-11-14 18:30:44+00","detail":"Ran it first week in November 2020 had strainer above baby chimp in a bad spot especially on the river left side of the strainer. Then there was about a half a dozen more river wide strainers all big trees. The rest were easier to avoid than the one above baby chimp but would be challenging at high flows. If anyone wants to get together and cut these trees out I'm all for it contact me and let me know.","user":{"name":"David Fulcher"}},{"id":"101249921","title":null,"post_date":"2012-12-26 05:00:00+00","detail":"The previously mentioned strainer that Kemper spoke of in an earlier comment has been replaced with a giant 30\" old growth tree that will not be going anywhere anytime soon. However, this tree can be sees way upstream in plenty time to portage on the left. There's one other log a few miles downstream that can be easily portaged on the right also.","user":{"name":"Brandon Hughett"}},{"id":"101246479","title":null,"post_date":"2010-02-10 05:00:00+00","detail":"There is an inconveniently placed log at the end of some class II boogie water that finishes in the pool above the known slot seive. You are comitted to the boogie water before you notice it in the only passable channel. It can be boofed but it claimed swims from three of the five members of our party. There are no easy eddies available by the time you notice the log. We tried to extract it for about 45 minutes with no success. The bridge guage level was 2.5 when we put on and 2.0 when we took out. It may not be an issue at higher flows. We were granted permission to park at the house on the river right downstream side of the bridge. The resident said that the driveway is used as a turnaround for a school bus so weekday parking might be out of the question. Ask for permission. Be careful.","user":{"name":"Kemper Begley"}}],"photos":[{"id":"5872","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5696","description":"You can put-in on either side of the bridge. However, river right has a nice pull-off road and a fairly easy trail under the railroad bridge.","caption":"(RM) Put-In","poi_name":"Put-In","subject":"","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5872","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5872"}},{"id":"5875","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5696","description":"Peter Horne seal launches at Baby Gorilla. Very few portages are this easy and fun.","caption":"(RM) Seal Launch at Baby Gorilla","poi_name":"Baby Gorilla","subject":"Peter Horne","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5875","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5875"}},{"id":"3193","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5521","description":"Big tree on the left side of the first major rapid on Tiny Piney.  Probably could be avoided by a far right line. This was 12/2002 and the tree was rocking a little in the current so it will most likely wash out with a big rain and lodge somewheres else.","caption":"Stairstep Rapid Strainer","poi_name":"Stairstep Rapid","subject":"","author":"Marty Montana","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3193","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3193"}},{"id":"3191","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5522","description":"Todd \"B\" executes perfectly the dry line at Baby Gorilla. ITs an easy walk on the left with a seal launch into a deep pool.  May not be a great eddy above this drop at higher flows.","caption":"Dry Line at Baby Gorilla","poi_name":"Baby Gorilla","subject":"Todd \"B\" on the Dry Line","author":"Marty Montana","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3191","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3191"}},{"id":"3192","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5522","description":"Members of Team ELF (Keith Kugley, Todd \"B\", Paul Butler)investigate the left side of Baby Gorilla to ponder if its undercut.  General consensus was no, but it looked like a meat grinder.","caption":"Is it undercut?","poi_name":"Baby Gorilla","subject":"Team ELF","author":"Marty Montana","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3192","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3192"}},{"id":"3252","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5535","description":"about a 10 foot seal launch after the dry line at Baby Gorilla","caption":"seal launch after Baby Gorilla dry line","poi_name":"pool after Baby Gorilla","subject":"Andrew (team ELF)","author":"Paul Butler","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3252","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3252"}},{"id":"5876","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5696","description":"Duncan Cottrell runs one of the many unnamed class III rapids","caption":"(RM) One of many unnamed class IIIs","poi_name":"none","subject":"Duncan Cottrell","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5876","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5876"}},{"id":"3253","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5535","description":"undercut cliff on Tiney Piney","caption":"Tiney Piney undercut","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Butler","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3253","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3253"}},{"id":"5874","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5696","description":"Kirk Duchow prepairs to seal launch at Baby Gorilla. The portage is easy and the seal launch is fun. ","caption":"(RM) Baby Gorilla Portage","poi_name":"Baby Gorilla","subject":"Kirk Duchow","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5874","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5874"}},{"id":"5873","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5696","description":"Kirk Duchow running Stair Step","caption":"(RM) Stair Step","poi_name":"Stair Step","subject":"Kirk Duchow","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5873","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5873"}},{"id":"1719","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2532","description":"","caption":"Cul-de-sac","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Mike Whaley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1719","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1719"}},{"id":"1708","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2533","description":"For a higher-resolution copy of this picture, <a href=\"http://rapids.americanwhitewater.org/photos/?photoid=1397\">click here</a>.","caption":"Tiny Piney, TN","poi_name":"","subject":"Craig Rollins","author":"Andy Moore","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1708","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1708"}},{"id":"1359","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4052","description":"","caption":"G -spot","poi_name":"G - Spot","subject":"Kemper Begley","author":"Craig Rollins","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1359","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1359"}},{"id":"1358","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4053","description":"One Great Surf Wave","caption":"G -Spot","poi_name":"G - Spot","subject":"Craig Rollins","author":"Kemper Begley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1358","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1358"}},{"id":"1397","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4053","description":"Another beautifal Jan. day","caption":"Tiny Piney, TN","poi_name":"?","subject":"Craig Rollins","author":"Andy Moore","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1397","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1397"}},{"id":"12349","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4943","description":"A good medium low level for the run this rapid just gets better and better as it goes up.......","caption":"Wade Running Baby Chimp","poi_name":"Baby Chimp","subject":"Wade Harrison","author":"Craig Rollins","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/12349","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/12349"}},{"id":"3190","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5521","description":"Sort of an intimidating rapid.  Near the beginning of the run. Second major rapid on the river after Stairstep.","caption":"Baby Gorilla","poi_name":"Baby Gorilla","subject":"","author":"Marty Montana","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3190","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3190"}},{"id":"5877","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5696","description":"You can't beat the natural beauty of the Piney. Even if Milt is in the picture........","caption":"(RM) Scenic Piney","poi_name":"","subject":"Milt Aitken","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/5877","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/5877"}},{"id":"3194","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5522","description":"This a good shot of how cool some of the scenery is on this river.  Its worth a trip down just for that reason alone.  The river really contricts here and the rocks form a sort of Micro Gorge around this rapid.","caption":"Baby Gorilla Micro Gorge","poi_name":"Baby Gorilla","subject":"Paul Butler","author":"Marty Montana","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3194","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3194"}},{"id":"3244","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5535","description":"Todd B (Team ELF)in some boogie on the Tiney Piney.","caption":"Todd B (Team ELF) on the TIney Piney","poi_name":"some boogie","subject":"Todd B","author":"Paul Butler","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/3244","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3244"}}],"detail":{"id":"1783","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Put in from Hwy 70 outside Rockwood.\n\nPaddle out on White's Creek to Hwy 27.\n\nFrom Wade Harrison:\n\nIt has 2 class 4's on it, Baby Gorilla and Stairstep Rapid. Both are pretty close to the putin and easily walked. It has only one undercut, which is one of the first rapids on the run; when its over 1.5', you want even see it; it's covered.\n\nBut the gorge isn't the fun part. The play is unbelievable on that run at 4'. When you start to leave the gorge you hit Triple Hole, back to back to back holes that are deep and very playful. Then a few more class 3 stuff and fun holes and then its SURF CITY BABY where [White's Creek](http://americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1799/) comes in on the right. You just have to see it at high water to get the real feel for that run, for sure a big-water run. I have run it as high as 8+ feet (no washout level for this run--it only gets meaner) and the holes were crazy at that level. About...6', I would say, is a good cutoff for that run..that means something better is running if it's that high. Overall, its an awesome run that i miss a lot, pretty easy to boat-scout everthing,.\n\nFrom Andrew McClanahan 2002-12-19:\n\nThe put in for the Tiney Piney is actually on Highway 70. Put it in at the bridge over the creek; there is a small gravel road just before the bridge on the left, drive underneath the railroad bridge and put in there (This is just a couple of miles past Ozone Falls if you're headed toward Knoxville from Crossville). Take out is on White's Creek at Highway 27. (To get here, continue on 70 and turn right on 27, White's Creek will be the first bridge you come to, park on the right.) This is a short shuttle.\n\nAs of 12-14-02 there is a large tree at the bottom of the first class IV rapid (Stairstep?). Run far right or walk on the left. There is also another bad tree near the end of the first rapid after Baby Gorilla. Walk on the right. Baby Gorilla, you will know it when you get there. The river contricts down into a micro gorge. Walk this one on the right.\n\nThis run has great scenery. Most of the run is fun class three with several long low-grade slide-type rapids. Watch out for an undercut slot on river right a few rapids below Baby Gorilla, and a couple of more pesky trees. Whoever owns much of the land on river right has posted numerous 'no trespassing' signs on the upstream side of the trees, most likely with boaters in mind. When you get to the junction with White's Creek, it takes on more river characteristics, with rapids of mostly waves and some juicy holes.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"8.1","correlations":[{"reachID":"1783","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"2.5","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"10"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"TELLICO RIVER AT TELLICO PLAINS, TN","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"03518500","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"146","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"1.12","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"35.3620163","longitude":"-84.2790141"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/03518500","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/03518500/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":null,"comment":null,"status":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"1.12","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"levelFT","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["110061","115319"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-84.737874,35.853867],[-84.737885,35.853825],[-84.737919,35.853617],[-84.737919,35.853348],[-84.7